From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Berkay=20K=C3=B6sebay?= <brkosebay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 21:38:42 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] dt-bindings: gpio: apple,d2365-pmic-gpio: Describe the PMU
 event interrupt controller
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The D2365 latches 240 PMU events in a 30-byte read-to-clear block at 0x140
and signals any unmasked pending event on one active-low line to the AP.
Reading the block acknowledges every event, so the block needs a single
owner that hands events to the charger, the Smart Cover halls and later
consumers.

Describe the PMIC GPIO node as that owner: an interrupt controller whose
specifier is the PMU event number plus flags, fed by the PMU event line.
GPIO line N is event 0x30 + N. Interrupt 240, one past the last event, is
the line itself: its consumers run before the block is read, for the
charger's withdrawal. The line may come through interrupts-extended, as it
does on the iPads. The D2333 has no such description yet.

Signed-off-by: Berkay Kösebay <brkosebay@users.noreply.github.com>
[Orchard: from brkosebay/linux f5ce1082 and omarchy-ipad5 patches/sensors/smart-cover-r1/0001, 0003 (J71t ledger 5.1); the interrupt controller moves from the PMIC node to the GPIO node, covers every event instead of the two hall lines, and needs no opt-in property]
Co-developed-by: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Signed-off-by: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../bindings/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml  | 49 +++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 46 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml
index 6deb36dd7..72f3c6fef 100644
--- a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml
@@ -4,16 +4,31 @@
 $id: http://devicetree.org/schemas/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml#
 $schema: http://devicetree.org/meta-schemas/core.yaml#
 
-title: Apple T8010 PMIC GPIO controller
+title: Apple A9/A10 PMIC GPIO and event interrupt controller
 
 maintainers:
   - Paul Praschl <praschlpaul@g-p.at>
 
 description: |
-  The Dialog D2333 and D2365 PMICs used in Apple A10 devices provide 21 and
-  24 GPIOs respectively. The controller is accessed through the regmap
+  The Dialog D2333 and D2365 PMICs used in Apple A9 and A10 devices provide
+  21 and 24 GPIOs respectively. The controller is accessed through the regmap
   exported by the parent Apple I2C PMIC device.
 
+  The D2365 also latches 240 PMU events (charger, ADC, GPIO edges and
+  others) in one read-to-clear block and signals them on a single active-low
+  line to the AP. As an interrupt controller the node owns that block: the
+  interrupt specifier is the PMU event number and a flags cell. PMIC GPIO
+  line N is event 0x30 + N, so GPIO consumers get their interrupt through the
+  GPIO specifier as well. Events are latched; what triggers a GPIO event is
+  the line configuration left by the boot firmware, so only edge flags are
+  accepted.
+
+  Interrupt 240, one past the last event, is the event line itself. Its
+  consumers run on every assertion of the line, before the event block is
+  read, so that a consumer can act before the I2C read that tells which
+  event it was (the Lightning charger withdraws its input-current grant
+  there).
+
 properties:
   compatible:
     enum:
@@ -28,15 +43,40 @@ properties:
   gpio-reserved-ranges:
     maxItems: 3
 
+  interrupt-controller: true
+
+  "#interrupt-cells":
+    const: 2
+
+  interrupts:
+    maxItems: 1
+    description: PMU event output of the PMIC, active low.
+
 required:
   - compatible
   - gpio-controller
   - "#gpio-cells"
 
+dependencies:
+  interrupt-controller: ["#interrupt-cells"]
+
+allOf:
+  - if:
+      properties:
+        compatible:
+          const: apple,d2333-pmic-gpio
+    then:
+      properties:
+        interrupt-controller: false
+        "#interrupt-cells": false
+        interrupts: false
+
 additionalProperties: false
 
 examples:
   - |
+    #include <dt-bindings/interrupt-controller/irq.h>
+
     pmic {
         #address-cells = <1>;
         #size-cells = <1>;
@@ -45,6 +85,9 @@ examples:
             compatible = "apple,d2365-pmic-gpio";
             #gpio-cells = <2>;
             gpio-controller;
+            #interrupt-cells = <2>;
+            interrupt-controller;
+            interrupts = <38 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_LOW>;
         };
     };
 ...
